VeridianOS: libksysguard Formatter: binary input prefixes with metric units

GCC reported formatter/Formatter.cpp:478: variable 'inputOrder' set but not
used [-Wunused-but-set-variable].

This is a real bug left by upstream commit 9ddde229 ("Follow the user
preference when formatting byte values", Plasma 6.7). That commit made
unitOrder() return 1000 for byte units when the user picks the metric
dialect, and computed an inputOrder of 1024 for values that arrive with
binary prefixes (memory and process sensors report KiB) -- but never used
it. A value in KiB was converted with 1000 as if it were kB:

- 1048576 KiB (1 GiB, 1.07 GB) was shown as "1.0 GB" instead of "1.1 GB",
  and 500.0 KiB as "500.0 kB" instead of "512.0 kB";
- the automatic prefix was picked from the wrong magnitude (990 KiB,
  1.01 MB, shown as "990 kB");
- scaleDownFactor(), used for the chart range unit multipliers, returned
  1000 instead of 976.5625 for KiB to MB.

adjustedUnit() now uses the input magnitude for the absolute value;
formatNumber() and scaleDownFactor() share a unitScale() helper (divide by
the input magnitude of the source prefix, multiply by the display
magnitude of the target prefix); maximumLength() passes its samples in
the base unit. With the IEC and JEDEC dialects and for all other units
both magnitudes are the same and nothing changes. (Prepared for upstream
with a regression test, invent.kde.org/plasma/libksysguard.)

--- a/formatter/Formatter.cpp
+++ b/formatter/Formatter.cpp
@@ -467,6 +467,23 @@ static Unit unitBase(Unit unit)
     }
 }
 
+static int inputUnitOrder(Unit unit)
+{
+    const Unit baseUnit = unitBase(unit);
+    // Indepent of the user preference how it should be shown in the  UI the input byte values use "proper" magnitudes of 1024
+    if (baseUnit == Unit::UnitByte || baseUnit == Unit::UnitByteRate || baseUnit == Unit::UnitBitRate) {
+        return 1024;
+    }
+    return unitOrder(unit);
+}
+
+// The factor to divide a value in unit by to express it in adjusted, a unit with the same base unit
+static qreal unitScale(Unit unit, Unit adjusted)
+{
+    const Unit baseUnit = unitBase(unit);
+    return std::pow(unitOrder(unit), adjusted - baseUnit) / std::pow(inputUnitOrder(unit), unit - baseUnit);
+}
